Common Special Tokens for GPT

1. [PAD] - Padding token
2. [BOS] - Beginning of sequence token
3. [EOS] - End of sequence token
4. [MASK] - Mask token
5. [UNK] - Unknown token
6. [USER] - User token
7. [EVE] - EVE token
8. [NAME] - Name token
9. [TOKEN 1 - TOKEN INF] - These can be tokens such as age, gender, location, prefrence etc

For tokens not [start], [end], [user], [eve]. try to make the others <token>

1. Context Storage and Special Tokens:
Context can be stored at the time of generation: You can store information (like user names, preferences, or past conversation history) and inject it back into the model’s generation process using special tokens.
Special tokens are useful to guide the model when to use stored information. For example, tokens like <NAME>, <AGE>, or <LOCATION> can represent dynamic, context-dependent data. The model will learn to use them appropriately if trained correctly.
2. Pretraining and Fine-Tuning:
Pretrain on real-world data: This helps the model learn general language patterns, conversational structures, and task-specific knowledge. You can start with a general dialogue corpus like the Cornell Movie-Dialogs or Persona-Chat dataset.
Fine-tune with synthetic data: After pretraining, fine-tuning with your specific, synthetic data (which includes personalized tokens and context-specific examples) helps the model adapt to your specific use case. This data can include special tokens (e.g., <USER>, <BOT>, <NAME>) to make the model more aware of context and structure, improving its ability to handle dynamic, personalized conversations.
Final Steps:
Store dynamic context (like the user’s name) during inference and inject it into the conversation when appropriate.
Pretrain on real-world data: Gather dialogue data from publicly available datasets.
Fine-tune with synthetic data: Add special tokens and adjust the training process to ensure the model learns to handle dynamic context properly.
This approach should help your model become more conversationally coherent, adaptable to user-specific details, and more context-aware when generating responses.
